# 3. FIX THE HTTP 404 / SOFT-404 PROBLEM

This is a critical remaining issue.

The current Nginx configuration includes fallback logic similar to:

`try_files $uri $uri/ $uri/index.html /index.html;`

The React router can also fall back to the homepage when it encounters an unknown URL.

This can produce HTTP 200 responses for URLs that do not exist.

Fix this properly.

## Required behavior

| Request | Expected HTTP status |
|---|---|
| Homepage | 200 |
| Existing movie detail | 200 |
| Existing TV series | 200 |
| Valid TV episode | 200 |
| Existing tag with meaningful content | 200 |
| Invalid movie ID | 404 |
| Invalid TV ID | 404 |
| Invalid season/episode | 404 |
| Unknown tag | 404 |
| Unknown public route | 404 |
| Temporary TMDB failure without usable cache | 503 |
| Existing API functionality | Preserve its appropriate status |

Important:

A valid numeric TMDB ID does not automatically prove that a media title exists.

Validate the actual media type and returned TMDB entity.

Do not render an invented title from an invalid slug.

Do not convert an upstream timeout into a 404.

Do not return the homepage with HTTP 200 for unknown URLs.

Implement a proper custom 404 HTML page.

The 404 page should:
- Have a useful title.
- Include a clear H1.
- Include navigation back to TStream.
- Return HTTP 404.
- Use noindex.
- Not be inserted into any sitemap.

Do not use `error_page 404 /index.html` as a workaround.

